## The idea

Build each ordering one position at a time. For the next position, **try every number not yet used**, go deeper, then **undo** that choice and try the next. When all positions are filled, save a **copy** of the ordering.

## Step by step

1. Keep `used[]` and a list `current`.
2. If `current` is full, add a copy to the result.
3. Otherwise for each unused index i: mark it, add nums[i], recurse, then remove it and unmark it.

## Worked example

**Input:** nums = [1, 2, 3]

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| pick 1 | then 2 → then 3 → save [1,2,3] |
| undo 3, 2 | pick 3 → then 2 → save [1,3,2] |
| undo to the top | pick 2 → [2,1,3], [2,3,1] |
| pick 3 | [3,1,2], [3,2,1] |

**Result:** 6 orderings (3! = 6)

## Cost

O(n · n!) time — n! orderings, each copied in O(n). O(n) extra memory besides the output.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LeetCode 46 — every ordering of distinct numbers.
public static class Permutations {
    public static IList<IList<int>> All(int[] nums) {
        var result = new List<IList<int>>();
        if (nums == null) return result;
        var used = new bool[nums.Length];      // used[i]: nums[i] is already in the current ordering
        var current = new List<int>();
        Build(nums, used, current, result);
        return result;
    }

    private static void Build(int[] nums, bool[] used, List<int> current, List<IList<int>> result) {
        if (current.Count == nums.Length) {    // every number placed: save a COPY (current keeps changing)
            result.Add(new List<int>(current));
            return;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.Length; i++) {
            if (used[i]) continue;
            used[i] = true; current.Add(nums[i]);          // choose
            Build(nums, used, current, result);            // explore
            current.RemoveAt(current.Count - 1); used[i] = false;   // un-choose
        }
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Duplicates in the input?**

That is LeetCode 47 — sort, then skip an equal number whose previous copy is unused.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(n · n!) time: n! orderings, n to copy each. O(n) extra besides the output.

**Q: Swap-based version?**

Swap position i with each j ≥ i, recurse, swap back. No `used` array; output order differs.

**Q: Shared state?**

None outside the call. Each call has its own buffers.

**Q: n = 12.**

479 million orderings — do not store them. Generate the next one on demand (next-permutation in O(n)).

**Q: How would you test it?**

Count is n!, every result is distinct, every result uses each number once.

